## 2. Spec Axis — Canonical Imports, Toy Clones, Real I/O

### 2.1 Canonical import adoption

| Layer | `std/sec/*` | `std/core/ooda.oo` | `std-hw/*` |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| L2 OS (18 repos) | 18 of 18 repos | 0 | 18 of 18 repos |
| L1 kernel, consolidated (6 repos) | 6 of 6 repos, 8 canonical security pages each | 6 of 6 repos | 0 |
| L1 kernel, not yet consolidated (10 repos) | **0 of 10** | 0 | 0 |

The six consolidated kernel repositories — `oodrv-net` (`net/net_dma_ring.oo`), `oodrv-nvme` (`core/nvme_sq_cq.oo`),
`oodrv-virtio` (`core/vring_split.oo`), `ookernel-boot` (`core/elf_loader.oo`), `ookernel-hal-x86` (`x86/lapic_ioapic.oo`), and
`ookernel-sched` (`sched/upcall_queue.oo`) — import the canonical security pages
`std/sec/{capreg,warrant,attest,audit,blackbox,speculate,quorum,delegate}.oo` and the canonical turn engine `std/core/ooda.oo`,
and every one of them calls `oo_ooda_cycle` on a live runtime page. The other ten import none of them and carry local copies
instead.

### 2.2 The canonical PQC page has no L1 importer

`std/sec/pqc.oo` states that it is the canonical post-quantum primitive page that "every layer imports instead of carrying
copies", and it explicitly forbids toy prime moduli. It is imported by `std/core/ooda.oo` and by the L2 and L4/L7 consumers of
that engine, but **zero of the sixteen L1 repositories import it directly**. The L1 consumers reach it only transitively through
`std/core/ooda.oo`. This is reported as F4, because the page claims a wider adoption than the tree shows.

### 2.3 Toy clones (the `% 1000000007` pattern)

`rules.oot` §1.14 and the review charter ban `wire.oo`, `warrant.oo`, `attest.oo`, and `quorum/blackbox.oo` inside L1, and ban a
copied shared data structure across repositories.

Ten L1 repositories ship all four banned files — 40 pages. Thirty of those forty compute a MAC, an attestation digest, or a seal
with the toy modulus `1000000007` (three of the four files in each repository; `quorum/blackbox.oo` is the one that does not).

The pages are rename clones. `diff openOODA-kernel/oodrv-pci/wire.oo openOODA-kernel/oodrv-usb-hid/wire.oo` reports only the
repository name and the `drv_pci_` / `drv_usbhid_` function prefix; the arithmetic, including

```
let x: Int = raw % 1000000007;
h = (h * 1000003 + mag * 31) % 1000000007;
```

is identical. This is the Fowler duplicated-code smell and the banned template clone at the same time.

Three of the ten also carry a `persona/` subtree: `oodrv-display-gop`, `oodrv-pci`, and `ookernel-mm`.

The six consolidated kernel repositories are clean: 0 banned pages, 0 `persona/` subtrees.

### 2.4 Real I/O

**L2 holds real I/O in 18 of 18 repositories.** Every repository calls `fs_read_dir` or `fs_read_file` under `FsReadCap` outside
its test tree. `ooinit` and `oosvc-mgr` scan a real directory tree through `svcmgr/procscan.oo` with `fs_path_exists`,
`fs_is_dir`, `fs_read_dir`, and `fs_read_file`, and fail closed with scanner codes 20 to 23. No L2 repository resolves a path
through `sys_exec` to a host binary.

**Real socket I/O sits with the three network-owning daemons**, and it is real rather than declarative. `oollama` serves HTTP over
`BindCap` and `TcpCap` using `std/net/tcp_conn.oo`, `std/net/http_stream.oo`, and `std/net/http_server.oo`.
`oonetd/core/capreg.oo` and `oosysbus/core/capreg.oo` bind a real loopback listener through
`tcp_conn_bind(bind, "127.0.0.1", 15353)` and close it, and both fail closed with `Err("net")` when the bind fails. The
remaining fifteen L2 daemons are file and process personas that correctly declare no socket capability.

### 2.5 Hardcoded hostnames and addresses

No product `.oo` page in L0, L1, or L2 contains a fleet host name or a tailnet address. The reviewer-service occurrences in
`oodac` are comments, and `oodac/qa/emit_gap_inline_repro.oo` reads its endpoint from `OODA_REVIEWER` and fails closed when the
variable is unset. The CGNAT-range text in `std/net/egress.oo` is the CGNAT range in an egress policy, not a host address.
